# Point every Maven invocation from the repo root at .mvn/settings.xml, which
# declares the Maven Central snapshot repository. Without it the mvnflight core
# extension in .mvn/extensions.xml cannot be resolved: core extensions are fetched
# by Maven's bootstrap BEFORE the project POM is read, so only the settings file
# can add a repository in time, and the built-in `central` has snapshots disabled.
#
# The path is relative to the working directory, so this works for `./mvnw ...`
# run at the repo root (which is how the build is meant to be run, and what CI
# does). Anything invoking Maven from elsewhere should pass the absolute path
# with -s itself.
#
# Behind a corporate mirror or proxy: -s REPLACES ~/.m2/settings.xml instead of
# merging with it, so delete this file and copy the central-snapshots repository
# from .mvn/settings.xml into your own ~/.m2/settings.xml.
#
# Comment lines in maven.config require Maven 3.9+ — the version the wrapper pins,
# and the version the GitHub-hosted runners ship, which is what parses this file in
# CI (the workflows call the runner's `mvn`, not ./mvnw).
#
# One argument per LINE. Maven 3.9 treats each line as a single argument and does
# NOT split it on whitespace, so `-s .mvn/settings.xml` on one line would be read as
# the option -s with the value " .mvn/settings.xml" — leading space included — and
# the build dies with "The specified user settings file does not exist". Use the
# attached --settings=<path> form instead.
--settings=.mvn/settings.xml
